      <EssayHeader slug="tenure-without-persons" />

      <p class="e-thesis"><span class="e-thesis-label">In short:</span> Tenure is a one-way device, and it protects whatever it happens to be holding. In today's firms the thing that gets tenure is no longer a person but a rule, and the first permanent institution on Mars will probably be a checklist.</p>
      <div class="e-body">
        <p class="mb-6">In 1990 Roger Kimball published <em>Tenured Radicals: How Politics Has Corrupted Our Higher Education</em>. The book argued that the American humanities had been captured by people whose political commitments came before their scholarship. It is a polemic. It was meant to wound, and it did.</p>
        <p>The U.S. Department of Education catalogued it. The record is <a href="https://eric.ed.gov/?id=ED328110" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="underline hover:opacity-70 t-amber">ED328110</a> in ERIC, the federal education research database. Publication type: Books; Opinion Papers. Two hundred and four pages. Harper and Row, ten East Fifty-third Street, New York, eighteen dollars and ninety-five cents. Full text not available. Descriptors assigned: College Faculty. Curriculum. Curriculum Development. Educational Trends. Feminism. Higher Education. Humanities Instruction. Ideology. Liberal Arts. Marxism. Moral Values. North American Culture. Political Influences. Political Issues. Politics of Education. Role of Education. Technical Writing. Trend Analysis. Western Civilization.</p>
      </div>
      <figure class="my-14">
