      <h2 class="e-h2">The Ratchet Has No Politics</h2>
      <div class="e-body">
        <p class="mb-6">Before the irony, the mechanism. The phrase everyone remembers has been misread for thirty-six years, both by the people who loved it and by the people who hated it.</p>
        <p class="mb-6">"Tenured radicals" does not describe an ideology. It describes a change of state, like water freezing. The adjective does almost all the work. What alarmed Kimball was never the content of the beliefs, which had circulated harmlessly in the same buildings for two decades. It was that the beliefs had gained a permanent claim on the institution's resources, through a procedure that nobody who disagreed could reverse.</p>
        <p class="mb-6">Tenure is a ratchet, a device that turns one way and cannot be turned back. The American version was first argued for in the 1915 Declaration of Principles of the newly founded American Association of University Professors (AAUP) and formalized in the 1940 Statement of Principles, issued by the AAUP with the Association of American Colleges. The 1915 Declaration described the university as an intellectual experiment station where new ideas could germinate and their fruit be allowed to ripen until, perchance, it became part of the accepted intellectual food of the nation. The threat it was built against was the trustee: the wealthy donor or political appointee who would fire a professor for reaching an unwelcome conclusion. It worked. It is one of the more successful institutional inventions of the twentieth century, and it worked exactly as specified.</p>
        <p>That is the problem, and the whole problem. A device that protects a scholar from dismissal for their views protects every scholar from dismissal for their views. It cannot be built any other way, because a version that discriminated on content would be the very thing tenure exists to prevent. So the ratchet turns for whoever is holding it when it turns. It has no politics. It has a direction.</p>
      </div>
      <h2 class="e-h2">Waved in Their Faces</h2>
      <div class="e-body">
        <p class="mb-6">The best demonstration of this was written by someone who very much did not want it to be true and published it anyway. That is to his credit, and it is why the document is worth reading now.</p>
        <p class="mb-6">On November 21, 1991, Michael P. McDonald, president and general counsel of the Center for Individual Rights (CIR), spoke at the Heritage Foundation. The talk was published the following year as <a href="https://static.heritage.org/1992/pdf/hl371.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="underline hover:opacity-70 t-amber">Heritage Lecture 371</a>, "Defending Academic Freedom." CIR had just set up an Academic Freedom Defense Fund, offering free legal help to students and professors on the wrong end of what was then universally called political correctness. McDonald summed up their approach in five words borrowed from the public interest litigator John Banzhaf: "sue the bastards."</p>
        <p class="mb-6">They had been winning.</p>
        <ul>
          <li>A Georgetown law student wrote in the student paper that the school applied a double racial standard in admissions. He was charged with breach of confidentiality, settled on favorable terms, and graduated.</li>
          <li>A fraternity at George Mason was punished for an offensive charity contest. It won on First Amendment grounds before Judge Claude Hilton.</li>
          <li>A professor at Delaware had been blocked by her university from accessing an outside foundation's research money. She got the ban voided in arbitration.</li>
          <li>At the City College of New York, the philosopher Michael Levin had written off campus about race and intelligence. The college responded by convening a committee on whether his views amounted to conduct unbecoming a scholar, and by running shadow sections of his classes so students could avoid him. Levin won before Judge Kenneth Conboy, who found the college had punished him solely for his expressed ideas and ordered it to stop harassing him.</li>
        </ul>
        <p class="mb-6">Then McDonald told the room what happened next, in a section he headed with a warning that victories may come back to haunt you and be used against you.</p>
        <p class="mb-6">At the same college, in the same period, Leonard Jeffries chaired the department of African-American studies. He was under mounting pressure over a speech in Albany that summer and over material he had long distributed in class, and his reappointment as chairman was thought to be in danger. City College officials, McDonald reports, literally waved a copy of the Levin decision in the faces of those demanding action, and said their hands were tied. Jeffries was reconfirmed, for a shortened term.</p>
        <p class="mb-6">Consider the shape of that. A conservative public interest firm builds a tool to protect a professor from an administration that wanted him gone for his opinions. Within weeks the same administration picks up the tool and uses it to protect the professor those litigants regarded as the worst actor on that campus. It does so while claiming, with what McDonald called appalling but predictable disingenuousness, that it had no choice. Both professors sued the same college president. The two case names differ only in the plaintiff.</p>
        <p>McDonald did not pretend this away, and his conclusion is the most durable thing in the lecture. Judicial protection of the principle of academic freedom, he wrote, does very little to improve the quality of what is being done in the name of academic freedom. That is a man watching his own tool work perfectly and produce a result he hated, and understanding why. The tool did not care what it protected. It had to be that way. That is what made it a tool rather than a faction.</p>
      </div>
      <figure class="my-14">
