      <h2 class="e-h2">Name the Thing You Cannot Undo</h2>
      <div class="e-body">
        <p class="mb-6">The instinct at this point is to ask how to keep the wrong ideas out. That instinct should be abandoned, because the record is clear that it cannot be done.</p>
        <p class="mb-6">Kimball's side could not do it. They were sophisticated, well advised, and correct about the specific abuses they litigated. Their own tool turned in their hands within a single semester, in the same building, against the same defendant. If a firm of lawyers who spent their careers thinking about exactly this problem could not build a ratchet that only turned their way, then a policy committee's confidence that it can screen for the right permanent beliefs is not a plan. It is a mood.</p>
        <p class="mb-6">The question that can be answered is simpler: which of your rules is revocable, by whom, on what evidence, and how would you find out? Any institution should be able to produce, on request, a list of the things it cannot undo, with the name of whoever could undo each one if it could be undone. Almost none can. The list exists in every case and is longer than management believes. It gets discovered the way City College discovered it: late, in public, when somebody waves a copy of it in your face.</p>
        <p class="mb-6">So write the list. Do it on a campus, in a firm, and above all in the first eighteen months of a settlement, when everything still looks provisional and nothing is. Mark each item with what would have to be true for it to be reversed. If the honest answer for an item is "nothing," then that item is now part of your constitution, and you should say so out loud. That is what a constitution is: not the document that says what you believe, but the document that lists what you have agreed not to reopen.</p>
        <p>And keep the record, <a href="https://eric.ed.gov/?id=ED328110" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="underline hover:opacity-70 t-amber">ED328110</a>, somewhere you can see it. A man wrote a book accusing an institution of being unable to tolerate criticism. The institution catalogued the book, assigned it nineteen descriptors, listed its price, declined to hold the full text, and moved on to the next item. Nobody suppressed anything, and nobody had to. The apparatus was never going to argue with him. It did not need to win, because it was still going to be there in 2026, indexing, when everybody involved in the argument was gone.</p>
      </div>
      <EssayFooter slug="tenure-without-persons" />

